First generate kappa from Yukawa and string interactions in the kappa directory, and then convert these files into the input tables (containing kappa and its derivative with respect to energy) for the Langevin code in the kappa_fnc directory.

1. About kappa directory：

kappa-yukawa.dat 4 columns in the file：temperature Te, momentum p1, energy e1, kappa
Go into directory: /kappa/Yukawa-term/collision-rate/charm/
In terminal, run ./hqcompile.sh
run ./a.out
Get kappa-yukawa.dat

kappa-string.dat 4 columns in the file：temperature Te, momentum p1, energy e1, kappa
Go into directory: /kappa/string-term-NO-rsquare/collision-rate/charm
In terminal, run ./hqcompile.sh
run ./a.out
Get kappa-yukawa.dat

2. About kappa_fnc directory

For generating kappa_tp-y.dat, kappa_tp-s.dat, and kappa_tp-sy.dat
For example, kappa_tp-y.dat 4 columns in the file：temperature Te, momentum p1, kappa, d(kappa)/d(E)
These files contain the tables of kappa and its derivative with respect to E

Go into directory: /kappa-fnc/
(1) read kappa-yukawa.dat in main.f (only keep contribution from Yukawa)
In terminal, run ./driver.sh
run ./a.out
Get kappa_tp-y.dat
(2) read kappa-string.dat in main.f (only keep contribution from string)
In terminal, run ./driver.sh
run ./a.out
Get kappa_tp-s.dat
(3) read kappa-yukawa.dat and kappa-string.dat in main.f (take the sum of contributions from Yukawa and string)
In terminal, run ./driver.sh
run ./a.out
Get kappa_tp-sy.dat

The main.f file needs to be slightly adjusted for each case